{"entries":{"chapter-8-study-principles-of-geriatric-assessment|Chapter 8 Study: Principles of Geriatric Assessment":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Extend disease-focused assessment to cognition, mood, function, frailty, social support, finances, environment, spirituality and care preferences (p117).":["u3"],"copy|Instruments support rather than replace clinical judgment. Individual clinician assessment and interdisciplinary comprehensive geriatric assessment (CGA) are related but distinct (p117).":["u4"],"copy|Tailor assessment to the setting: home visits reveal environmental risks; nursing-home assessment emphasizes nutrition and self-care rather than shopping or meal preparation (p118).":["u5"],"copy|Weight loss above 4% in the previous year prompts evaluation; obtain baseline height and weight and weigh patients at subsequent visits (p120).":["u6"],"copy|Observe walking and chair rise. Timed Up and Go uses a 3-m walk; over 12 seconds warrants further evaluation (p121).":["u7"],"copy|Cognitive screens can alter the probability of dementia but do not diagnose it; normal results do not completely exclude it (p121).":["u8"],"copy|Ask about depressed mood and anhedonia over 2 weeks; follow a positive brief screen with fuller assessment (p123).":["u9"],"copy|The chapter reports PHQ-9 above 10 with 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity; for dementia, caregiver-rated Cornell score at least 6 has 93% sensitivity and 97% specificity (p123).":["u10"],"copy|Distinguish basic self-care, instrumental independent-living tasks and advanced activities; new functional change should prompt diagnostic evaluation (p123).":["u11"],"copy|Combine reported function with observed performance when useful. Function reflects disease in the context of environment and support (p123).":["u12"],"copy|Establish who helps with specific tasks, whether help is paid and who could assist during illness. Financial strain can present as nonadherence, weight loss or apparent neglect (p124).":["u13"],"copy|CGA includes physical health, function, psychological health and socioenvironmental factors (p128).":["u14"],"copy|Its essential sequence is targeting appropriate patients, assessment with recommendations, then implementation and adherence; assessment alone does not ensure benefit (p128).":["u15"],"copy|A positive screen is a starting point for clinical evaluation, not a substitute diagnosis or an automatic drug indication (p117, p121).":["u16"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u17"],"figure-8-2-book-image|Figure 8-2 — book image":["u18"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Figure 8-2, p.124":["u19","u33"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u20"],"copy|CHAPTER 8s / HAZZARD":["u23"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 8 - Principles of Geriatric Assessment; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u24"],"copy|Scope of assessment":["u25"],"copy|High-yield screening":["u26"],"copy|Function & support":["u27"],"copy|Management & exam traps":["u28"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u29"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u30"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u32"],"copy|Two past-exam questions cite printed pages in Chapter 8.":["u32"],"copy|The chapter reports PHQ-9 above 10 with 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity; for dementia, it reports 93% sensitivity and 97% specificity at a Cornell score of at least 6 (p123). In the original Cornell instrument, the clinician rates symptoms using information from the patient and an informant. Alexopoulos et al., 1988.":["u10"],"copy|Levels 7–9. At 7, the person depends on others for personal care but can be relatively stable. At 8, complete dependence accompanies approaching the end of life, with little capacity to recover from even a minor illness. Level 9 describes an expected survival under six months in someone not otherwise living with severe frailty (Figure 8-2, p124). Terminal illness and extreme functional dependence answer different questions. Hazzard 8e, pp. 123–124.":["u42"],"copy|Observe walking and chair rise. Timed Up and Go uses a 3-m walk; over 12 seconds warrants further evaluation in the prose; Table 8-3 reports a >15-second test cutpoint (p121).":["u7"],"copy|The chapter reports PHQ-9 above 10 with 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity; the chapter says caregivers can complete the Cornell scale in dementia and reports 93% sensitivity and 97% specificity at a Cornell score of at least 6 (p123). Current practice (not the book): In the original Cornell instrument, the clinician rates symptoms using information from the patient and an informant. Alexopoulos et al., 1988.":["u10"],"copy|Table 8-3 on the same page reports test performance using a different cutpoint, over 15 seconds.":["u197"],"copy|128–131":["u198"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ASCO recommends geriatric assessment to identify vulnerabilities in older adults receiving systemic cancer therapy.":["u199"],"copy|ASCO 2023 guideline.":["u120"],"copy|CHAPTER 8 / HAZZARD":["u23"],"copy|September 2024 Q92":["u21"],"copy|June 2026 Q38":["u22","u31"],"principles-of-geriatric-assessment|Principles of Geriatric Assessment":["u200"],"function-and-mobility|Function and mobility":["u201"],"frailty-and-the-level-of-help-a-person-needs|Frailty and the level of help a person needs":["u39"],"chair-rise-walking-and-falls|Chair rise, walking and falls":["u44"],"cognition-mood-and-sensory-loss|Cognition, mood and sensory loss":["u202"],"hearing-vision-and-decisions-about-screening|Hearing, vision and decisions about screening":["u43"],"function-mood-and-cognition-at-the-visit|Function, mood and cognition at the visit":["u46"],"support-and-goals|Support and goals":["u203"],"comprehensive-geriatric-assessment|Comprehensive geriatric assessment":["u204"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u205"],"copy|Ask about depressed mood and loss of interest over 2 weeks (p123).":["u206"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u207"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u208"],"copy|Begin with what the person can do at home.":["u209"],"copy|Function, cognition, sensory loss and support determine which findings change the care plan.":["u210"],"copy|Ask for the usual day before assigning a Clinical Frailty Scale score.":["u49"],"copy|Someone who lives alone can still depend on another person for shopping, bathing or dressing.":["u50"],"copy|Conversely, a walker can make a transfer independent.":["u51"],"copy|Ask separately about outside activities, housekeeping, bathing and dressing.":["u52"],"copy|Age alone cannot assign the score.":["u53"],"copy|The current nine-level scale appears in Figure 8-2.":["u54"],"copy|The older seven-level scale summarized in Table 42-3 also describes level 6 as requiring help with both instrumental and basic activities.":["u55"],"copy|Figure 8-2, p.":["u56"],"copy|124 · Table 42-3, p.":["u57"],"copy|621.":["u58"],"copy|Levels 1–4.":["u211"],"copy|At 1, the person is robust and exercises regularly.":["u212"],"copy|At 2, the person is fit and may be active only occasionally.":["u213"],"copy|At 3, medical problems are controlled but regular activity may be limited to ordinary walking.":["u214"],"copy|At 4, symptoms slow or restrict activities while daily independence is preserved.":["u215"],"copy|Levels 5–6.":["u216"],"copy|At 5, help is needed with higher-order tasks such as finances, transportation or heavy housework.":["u217"],"copy|At 6, assistance extends to outside activities and housekeeping, commonly bathing and sometimes parts of dressing.":["u218"],"copy|The centenarian needs someone to go out with him and help wash and put on shoes.":["u219"],"copy|These details distinguish 6 from a slower independent person or someone needing instrumental help alone.":["u220"],"copy|Levels 7–9.":["u221"],"copy|At 7, the person depends on others for personal care but can be relatively stable.":["u222"],"copy|At 8, complete dependence accompanies approaching the end of life, with little capacity to recover from even a minor illness.":["u223"],"copy|Level 9 describes an expected survival under six months in someone not otherwise living with severe frailty (Figure 8-2, p124).":["u224"],"copy|Terminal illness and extreme functional dependence answer different questions.":["u225"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, pp.":["u226","u228","u232","u233","u238","u244","u248","u251","u252"],"copy|123–124.":["u227"],"copy|How other instruments differ.":["u59"],"copy|Reduced physiologic reserve increases vulnerability to falls, fractures, hospitalization and disability.":["u60"],"copy|The physical phenotype assesses weight loss, weakness, exhaustion, slow walking and low activity.":["u61"],"copy|Accumulated-deficit approaches consider a person's burden of health deficits.":["u62"],"copy|The VES-13 asks about age, perceived health, physical tasks and functional difficulties.":["u63"],"copy|Choose the measure to suit the decision.":["u64"],"copy|With early weakness or slower gait, investigate disease and address strength, activity and nutrition while the patient can still participate.":["u65"],"copy|Tables 8-5 and 8-6, pp.":["u66"],"copy|125–126.":["u67"],"copy|Look at what the patient actually does.":["u94"],"copy|In the June 2023 photograph, arms are crossed in front of the chest and the chair is immediately behind the standing patient.":["u95"],"copy|A chair stand tests the ability to lift the body using the legs.":["u96"],"copy|Arms crossed prevent pushing off.":["u97"],"copy|A static balance test uses increasingly challenging foot positions.":["u98"],"copy|A gait-speed test times walking a measured distance.":["u99"],"copy|Timed Up and Go requires getting up, walking three metres, turning, returning and sitting.":["u100"],"copy|The chapter's text recommends further assessment when this sequence takes longer than 12 seconds.":["u101"],"copy|Related passage, p.":["u102"],"copy|121.":["u103"],"copy|From a fall to an intervention.":["u104"],"copy|Ask whether a fall caused injury, whether two or more falls occurred within the year and whether gait or balance causes fear of falling.":["u105"],"copy|With a positive history assess gait, strength, standing blood pressure, vision, medicines, feet, footwear and the home.":["u106"],"copy|Chair stands, standing balance and gait speed together make up the Short Physical Performance Battery.":["u107"],"copy|Slowness identifies a need to examine the limiting step.":["u108"],"copy|It does not reveal the cause by itself.":["u109"],"copy|120–121, Table 8-3.":["u111"],"copy|Instruments support rather than replace clinical judgment.":["u229"],"copy|Individual clinician assessment and interdisciplinary comprehensive geriatric assessment (CGA) are related but distinct (p117).":["u230"],"copy|Figure 8-2":["u231"],"copy|An office hearing screen can precede formal audiometry.":["u68"],"copy|Ask whether hearing has become difficult.":["u69"],"copy|A positive answer warrants referral for audiologic evaluation.":["u70"],"copy|A whisper test or a handheld tone-emitting otoscope is another way to screen.":["u71"],"copy|In the whisper test, stand behind the patient, occlude the opposite ear and ask the patient to repeat the whispered material.":["u72"],"copy|Repeating fewer than half correctly is an abnormal result in this protocol.":["u73"],"copy|Addressing hearing loss matters because it affects conversation, social participation and the assessment of cognition.":["u74"],"copy|The chapter describes insufficient evidence for a universal asymptomatic screening recommendation.":["u75"],"copy|Hearing assessment, p.":["u76"],"copy|119.":["u77"],"copy|Why the other parts of the screening question differ.":["u78"],"copy|The book describes fasting glucose or A1C screening after age 65.":["u79"],"copy|For an older woman considering mammography, model-based cost-effectiveness at a life expectancy of at least 9.5 years is distinct from a recommendation for an individual.":["u80"],"copy|Discuss expected survival, burdens of a false positive and preferences around ten years.":["u81"],"copy|Colonoscopy is one colorectal screening method alongside stool testing and flexible sigmoidoscopy.":["u82"],"copy|A positive stool blood test requires diagnostic follow-up.":["u83"],"copy|Breast and colorectal screening, p.":["u84"],"copy|161 · Diabetes screening, p.":["u85"],"copy|1561.":["u86"],"copy|Vision.":["u87"],"copy|Measure corrected visual acuity with the patient's glasses.":["u88"],"copy|In the chapter's Snellen procedure, a patient standing 20 feet away fails if unable to read all the letters on the 20/40 line.":["u89"],"copy|An abnormal office result directs further assessment.":["u90"],"copy|Ask about the impact on reading, medicines, mobility and falls.":["u91"],"copy|118–119.":["u93"],"copy|Separate what a person can do from what another person does for them.":["u140"],"copy|Ask who helps, with which part of the task and why.":["u141"],"copy|Bathing, dressing, toileting, continence, grooming, feeding and transferring are basic activities.":["u142"],"copy|Shopping, transportation, telephone use, meals, housework, laundry, managing medicines and finances are instrumental.":["u143"],"copy|Occupational, recreational and social roles are advanced activities.":["u144"],"copy|Giving up a valued role or finding a task increasingly difficult can precede outright dependence.":["u145"],"copy|Whenever possible combine a history from the patient and family with observed performance, especially during recovery from acute illness.":["u146"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, pp. 118 and 123.":["u147"],"copy|Cognition and mood give different clues to the same loss of function.":["u148"],"copy|The chapter distinguishes uncertain benefit from universal cognitive screening from a clear reason to assess when a patient or informant describes change.":["u149"],"copy|A brief test changes the likelihood of dementia.":["u150"],"copy|Abnormal results warrant assessment of memory, language, executive and visuospatial function together with everyday function.":["u151"],"copy|Fluctuation and inattention 